# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 1: Introduction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch1 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:label "ch:intro" ;
    doc:title "Introduction" .

:ch1_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "Thesis statement" .

:ch1_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch1_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """This paper consolidates two sources that instantiate the same
admit/manufacture/receipt pattern -- $\\Act=\\muop(\\Adm)$, the Chatman
equation of the keystone paper \\cite{keystone} -- at fleet scale, on
opposite sides of one boundary. \\texttt{docs/thesis/04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}
argues that admitting and re-admitting a fleet of computational agents
cannot be a matter of comprehension: it must be mechanized into a
decidable oracle, a branchless byte-level construction, and a receipting
invariant that bounds how large a claim is allowed to be. Chapters
\\ref{ch:gap}--\\ref{ch:antibody} instantiate that source.
\\texttt{docs/thesis/synthesis\\_thesis.tex} argues the complementary
half: the supervision structure that keeps such a fleet running --
which nodes restart together, which failures are named in advance,
which recovery claims are actually measured rather than assumed -- can
itself be derived from the plan, not hand-authored. Chapters
\\ref{ch:topology}--\\ref{ch:scope} instantiate that source. Together
they cover one lifecycle: admit a fleet, then keep it running when
parts of it fail.""" .

:ch1_s0_p1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch1_s0 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Every paragraph below carries a machine-checkable
\\texttt{doc:register} value, \\textsc{evidence-bound} or
\\textsc{speculative}, per \\texttt{packs/doc-pack/ontology.ttl}. An
evidence-bound paragraph names the artifact its claim traces to: a
corpus label, a source file, or a receipts-file line. A speculative
paragraph is motivated by evidence-bound results but is not itself
derived from or checked against them, and is never left unmarked next
to an evidence-bound claim in the same section. Chapter~\\ref{ch:planetary}
and the second section of Chapter~\\ref{ch:fable} are wholly speculative
and are titled as such. Three further sections carry individually
marked speculative paragraphs inside otherwise evidence-bound chapters
-- the borrowed-discipline-lineage section of Chapter~\\ref{ch:intro}
(titled ``(speculative)'' in its own heading), the wall-time estimate
of Section~\\ref{sec:wall-time-estimate} in Chapter~\\ref{ch:gap}, and
the two order-of-magnitude estimates of the sweep-time section in
Chapter~\\ref{ch:agent8} -- each set off with an explicit
\\textbf{Speculative.} marker and never left ambiguous next to the
evidence-bound arithmetic it sits beside.""" .

:ch1_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch1 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:label "sec:cited-once" ;
    doc:title "What is cited from the keystone, not re-derived" .

:ch1_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch1_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Two results recur across this pair of sources but belong,
proved once, to earlier papers in the series, and are cited rather than
re-derived here. First, Rice's theorem specialized to the observation
space -- for any non-trivial semantic property $P$ of what an
observation denotes, $\\{o\\in\\Obs:P(o)\\}$ is undecidable -- is proved
in \\texttt{docs/thesis/00\\_foundations.tex} (label \\texttt{thm:rice})
and restated at \\texttt{docs/thesis/projection\\_thesis.tex} (same
label); it is the reason admission must be a mechanical retraction and
not a semantic decision, and Chapter~\\ref{ch:oracle} below only draws
the operational consequence for oracle construction. Second, the
Comprehension--Verification Gap theorem -- boundary verification cost is
$O(\\CL)$ while comprehension cost grows without bound with the
interior -- is proved in \\texttt{docs/thesis/projection\\_thesis.tex}
(label \\texttt{thm:gap}, chapter ``The Two Cost Regimes and the
Comprehension--Verification Gap'') \\cite{keystone}. Chapter~\\ref{ch:gap}
below instantiates that theorem with one measured fleet-scale number; it
does not reprove it.""" .

:ch1_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch1 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "A borrowed discipline, briefly (speculative)" .

:ch1_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch1_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\textbf{Speculative.} \\texttt{docs/thesis/synthesis\\_thesis.tex}
motivates its derived-supervision design by analogy to three prior
disciplines, kernel-admitted as \\texttt{lineage} statements in
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-pilot/formalization\\_receipts.jsonl}
and \\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-lake/mathlib\\_migration\\_receipts.jsonl}
(both \\texttt{verified}, kernel 4.31.0), but a kernel admission of a
lineage statement certifies only that the analogy was formalized as
declared, not that the analogy is load-bearing for any claim in this
paper -- these are historical remarks, condensed to one paragraph rather
than three separate chapters, and are not cited again below.
\\begin{lineage}[Erlang/OTP]\\label{lineage:armstrong}
Erlang/OTP made three moves that survive every re-examination: processes
are isolated so failure cannot spread by memory; supervisors -- not the
failing code -- own the recovery decision; and restart strategies plus a
restart intensity turn `let it crash' into a bounded, structured
discipline. What OTP does not do is derive the tree: a human writes the
supervision hierarchy, and the crash space lives in the programmer's
head.
\\end{lineage}
\\begin{lineage}[Instrument design]\\label{lineage:instruments}
The LHC does not record every collision; its trigger system encodes, in
advance, the signatures of events worth keeping. The EHT did not
photograph a black hole; it reconstructed one from lawful projections
gathered by an instrument designed around a predicted signal. The
discipline common to both: enumerate the event geometry before the
event, instrument for exactly those signatures, and treat anything
outside the geometry as a first-class finding -- never as noise to be
discarded silently.
\\end{lineage}
\\begin{lineage}[The eight-bounded actor lineage]\\label{lineage:actorlineage}
The 8-bounded actor lineage (CNS $\\to$ BitActor $\\to$ ByteActor $\\to$
knhk): CNS fixed the doctrine 8T/8H/8M; ByteActor V3 productized it with
64-byte crystal envelopes and bounded SPSC rings; knhk carried the
doctrine into Rust with a branchless TickBudget, a ChatmanBounded
assertion, and an R1/W1/C1 runtime-class taxonomy. The lineage's one
persistent gap never closed: classification without actuation --
nothing in the constellation restarts, re-admits, or closes the loop.
\\end{lineage}
Chapters~\\ref{ch:topology}--\\ref{ch:geometry} below are this project's
own answer to the gap the third lineage names.""" .

:ch1_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch1 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:title "Structure of this document" .

:ch1_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch1_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Chapters~\\ref{ch:gap}--\\ref{ch:antibody} instantiate
\\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}: the measured
comprehension--verification ratio at one instance scale
(Chapter~\\ref{ch:gap}), the decidable-oracle discipline for mechanical
trust (Chapter~\\ref{ch:oracle}), the agent8 status byte and its
branchless fleet-admission arithmetic (Chapter~\\ref{ch:agent8}), the
planetary-scale Fermi extrapolation of admission demand and supply,
explicitly speculative (Chapter~\\ref{ch:planetary}), the antibody
clause bounding claim magnitude by receipting mechanism
(Chapter~\\ref{ch:antibody}), and -- despite its position later in this
document, among the chapters below -- the retrofit applier and
autonomic reconciliation tooling that apply this discipline to a
running fleet (Chapter~\\ref{ch:fleettool}: every definition and
proposition it states, including the worktree-isolation pair and the
residual-vector/gap-report apparatus, is sourced from
\\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}, not from
\\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}). Chapters~\\ref{ch:topology}--\\ref{ch:geometry}
and~\\ref{ch:cell},~\\ref{ch:fable}--\\ref{ch:scope}
instantiate \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}: supervision topology derived
from the plan's data-dependency DAG (Chapter~\\ref{ch:topology}), failure
geometry with a completeness-by-construction theorem
(Chapter~\\ref{ch:geometry}), the 10,000-member supervised-cell
fault-injection measurements (Chapter~\\ref{ch:cell}), the Fable
spec-driven-evaluation harness, evidence-bound in its mechanics and
explicitly speculative in two flagged statements, a proposition and a
remark (Chapter~\\ref{ch:fable}), the Merkle cell-rollup and write-ahead-log
constructions (Chapter~\\ref{ch:merkle}), and the named scope refusals
that keep the whole document's own claims inside what it can receipt
(Chapter~\\ref{ch:scope}). Chapter~\\ref{ch:conclusion} closes.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 2: The Comprehension--Verification Gap, Instantiated at Scale
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch2 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:label "ch:gap" ;
    doc:title "The Comprehension--Verification Gap, Instantiated at Scale" .

:ch2_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "Measured and estimated quantities" .

:ch2_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch2_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The keystone's Comprehension--Verification Gap theorem
(Section~\\ref{sec:cited-once} above, \\texttt{thm:gap} of
\\texttt{projection\\_thesis.tex}) is asymptotic: comprehension cost grows
without bound with the interior while boundary verification stays
$O(\\CL)$. \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex} instantiates it on one
concrete executed manufacture and, before quoting a ratio, disciplines
which of its inputs are measured and which are estimated -- a
methodological convention this whole document follows.
\\begin{definition}[Instance quantities]\\label{def:instance-q}
For an executed manufacture $\\sigma$: $T(\\sigma)$, the interior token
count (measured, a count of logged records); $\\CL$, the boundary field
count of the receipt projection $\\Proj(\\sigma)$ (a design constant);
$t_{\\chainH}$, the per-frame recomputation wall-time (estimated from
published hash throughput, not measured here).
\\end{definition}""" .

:ch2_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch2 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:label "sec:instance-gap-measured" ;
    doc:title "The measured ratio, not an estimate" .

:ch2_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch2_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Both $T(\\sigma)$ and $\\CL$ in the theorem below are the
measured/design-constant quantities of Definition~\\ref{def:instance-q},
not estimates; the ratio that follows is therefore itself a measured
consequence of the theorem's asymptotics applied to one instance, not a
new estimate layered on top of it.
\\begin{theorem}[Instance gap]\\label{thm:instance-gap}
For comprehension cost $\\mathrm{Comp}(\\sigma)=\\Theta(T(\\sigma))$ and
boundary-verification cost $\\mathrm{Ver}_\\partial(\\sigma)=\\Theta(\\CL)$,
the instance ratio is
$\\Theta(T(\\sigma)/\\CL)=\\Theta(2.5\\times10^{6}/4)\\approx 6.25\\times10^{5}$;
both inputs are measured/design constants, so the ratio is a measured
consequence, not an estimate.
\\end{theorem}""" .

:ch2_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch2 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "Boundary cost is invariant as the interior grows" .

:ch2_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch2_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The instance ratio of Theorem~\\ref{thm:instance-gap} would
be uninteresting if it shrank as manufactures grew; the next proposition
is what makes it a standing property of the frame schema rather than an
artifact of one instance's size.
\\begin{proposition}[Boundary invariance]\\label{prop:invariance}
For manufactures $\\{\\sigma_i\\}$ sharing the frame schema with
$T(\\sigma_i)\\to\\infty$, $\\mathrm{Ver}_\\partial(\\sigma_i)=\\Theta(\\CL)$ is
constant in $i$, and the estimated per-message wall-time
$\\CL\\cdot t_{\\text{cmp}}+c\\cdot t_{\\chainH}$ does not depend on
$T(\\sigma_i)$.
\\end{proposition}""" .

:ch2_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch2 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:label "sec:wall-time-estimate" ;
    doc:title "Per-message wall time: an estimate, not a measurement" .

:ch2_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch2_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\textbf{Speculative.} The constancy of
Proposition~\\ref{prop:invariance} is proved; the wall-clock seconds it
takes is not measured in this document and is only a Fermi estimate from
published BLAKE3 throughput \\cite{blake3}.
\\begin{estimate}[Wall time]\\label{est:wall}
At BLAKE3 throughput $1$--$3\\,\\mathrm{GB/s}$/core and a few-hundred-byte
frame, $t_{\\chainH}\\sim10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{s}$/frame; a four-field boundary
compare is $\\ll10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{s}$; per-message verification with
$c\\sim10$ spot frames costs $\\sim10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{s}$; these are
order-of-magnitude estimates, the constancy-in-$T$ point is the proved
Proposition~\\ref{prop:invariance}.
\\end{estimate}""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 3: Manufactured Trust: Decidable Oracles
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch3 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:label "ch:oracle" ;
    doc:title "Manufactured Trust: Decidable Oracles" .

:ch3_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:label "sec:oracle-necessity" ;
    doc:title "Why admission needs a decidable oracle" .

:ch3_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch3_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Rice's theorem specialized to observations (cited once,
Section~\\ref{sec:cited-once}) rules out deciding whether any concrete
implementation $f$ meets its specification $S$ by inspecting what $f$
means. \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex} names the mechanical
fallback: a decidable oracle, evaluated on a finite test set, that
witnesses correctness without deciding it in general.
\\begin{definition}[Decidable oracle]\\label{def:oracle}
For implementation $f:D\\to R$ and specification $S\\subseteq D\\times R$,
the correctness question is $Q_S(f)\\equiv\\forall x\\,(x,f(x))\\in S$; a
decidable oracle for $f$ on finite $X\\subseteq D$ is a total computable
predicate $\\Omega:X\\to\\{0,1\\}$ intended to witness $(x,f(x))\\in S$,
terminating on every $x\\in X$.
\\end{definition}""" .

:ch3_s0_p1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch3_s0 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{proposition}[Necessity of the oracle move]\\label{prop:necessity}
For $S$ a non-trivial semantic property, $Q_S(f)$ is undecidable; hence
no procedure decides $Q_S(f)$ for arbitrary $f$, and any mechanical
trust in $f$ must be trust in some $\\Omega$ evaluated on finite $X$,
plus an argument bounding residual risk on $D\\setminus X$.
\\end{proposition}
The remainder of this chapter is that argument, instantiated three ways:
differential agreement, admission-boundary fuzzing, and mutation
testing.""" .

:ch3_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch3 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Differential agreement between independent implementations" .

:ch3_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch3_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{definition}[Differential oracle]\\label{def:diff}
For $f,g:D\\to R$ targeting the same $S$, the differential oracle is
$\\Omega_{f,g}(x)=[f(x)=g(x)]$; a test passes at $x$ iff
$\\Omega_{f,g}(x)=1$.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{proposition}[False-accept bound]\\label{prop:diff-bound}
Under independent errors at rates $p_f,p_g$ with wrong outputs drawn
from $\\ge M$ distinguishable values with collision probability
$\\le1/M$: $\\Pr[\\Omega_{f,g}(x)=1\\wedge(x,f(x))\\notin S]\\le p_f p_g/M$;
over a corpus of size $n$ the probability some silent-agreement error
survives all $n$ tests is at most $np_f p_g/M$, and a fixed
silently-wrong input missed with per-input hit rate $q$ has miss
probability $(1-q)^n\\to0$.
\\end{proposition}
Two independent implementations agreeing is weak evidence per test and
strong evidence in aggregate; the bound is what turns ``they agree'' into
a quantified residual-risk statement rather than a shrug.""" .

:ch3_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch3 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "Admission-boundary fuzzing" .

:ch3_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch3_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{definition}[Fuzzing oracle]\\label{def:fuzz}
For the total admission retraction $\\adm:\\Obs\\rightharpoonup\\Adm\\cup\\{\\Rfsl\\}$,
the fuzzing oracle $\\Omega_\\partial$ maps generated $o\\in\\Obs$ to $1$
iff $\\adm(o)$ terminates and yields either an admitted $x\\in\\Adm$ with
well-formed artifact $\\muop(x)$ or a refusal $\\Rfsl$ carrying a category
in the eight-bucket taxonomy; $\\Omega_\\partial(o)=0$ iff $\\adm$ crashes,
diverges, or returns an unlabelled outcome.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{proposition}[Fuzzing soundness]\\label{prop:fuzz}
If $\\adm$ is total and terminating with codomain $\\Adm\\cup\\{\\Rfsl\\}$
and $\\Rfsl$ is refusal-with-category, then for every $o\\in\\Obs$,
$\\Omega_\\partial(o)=1$, and any observed $\\Omega_\\partial(o)=0$ is a
genuine defect in the retraction, not a property of the input.
\\end{proposition}
The soundness direction is what makes fuzzing worth running at all: a
crash found by admission-boundary fuzzing is never dismissible as
``the fuzzer generated a nonsense input'' -- $\\adm$ is total by
construction, so nonsense inputs are exactly what it is required to
refuse, not choke on.""" .

:ch3_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch3 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:title "The Mutant Kill Theorem" .

:ch3_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch3_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Mutation testing \\cite{demillo1978,jia2011} gives the third
oracle. \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex} formalizes a validator
as a staged pipeline and proves that, when every stage is sound and
complete, the stage a mutant is caught at is not incidental -- it is
determined by which invariant the mutation broke.
\\begin{definition}[Staged validator]\\label{def:staged}
A staged validator is a pipeline $V=(V_1,\\dots,V_k)$ where each stage
$V_i:\\mathcal{S}\\to\\{\\text{pass}\\}\\cup\\{\\text{reject}_i\\}$ tests a
decidable invariant $I_i\\subseteq\\mathcal{S}$; the pipeline accepts $s$
iff $s\\in\\bigcap_i I_i$, reporting on rejection the least $i$ with
$s\\notin I_i$. Stage $V_i$ is sound if
$V_i(s)=\\text{reject}_i\\Rightarrow s\\notin I_i$ and complete if
$s\\notin I_i\\Rightarrow V_i(s)=\\text{reject}_i$.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{definition}[Mutation, correct stage, kill]\\label{def:mut}
A mutation operator $m$ maps a valid subject $s^\\star\\in\\bigcap_i I_i$
to a mutant $m(s^\\star)$ violating a non-empty set of invariants; its
correct stage is $\\stg(m)=\\min\\{i:m(s^\\star)\\notin I_i\\}$; $m$ is
killed by $V$, written $\\Kill(m)=1$, iff $V$ rejects $m(s^\\star)$.
\\end{definition}""" .

:ch3_s3_p1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch3_s3 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{theorem}[Mutant Kill Theorem]\\label{thm:kill}
For a staged validator $V=(V_1,\\dots,V_k)$ in which every stage is sound
and complete, and a mutation operator $m$ with correct stage
$\\stg(m)$: $\\Kill(m)=1\\iff V_{\\stg(m)}$ rejects $m(s^\\star)$, and the
least rejecting stage reported by $V$ equals $\\stg(m)$.
\\end{theorem}
\\begin{corollary}[Diagnostic reading]\\label{cor:diag}
Under the Mutant Kill Theorem, the index of the killing stage certifies
which law-bearing invariant a mutation broke; a non-empty surviving set
that is not equivalent-mutants is a proof that the frame under-commits,
the Faithful Projection converse.
\\end{corollary}
Corollary~\\ref{cor:diag} is kernel-verified in both formalization lanes:
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-pilot/formalization\\_receipts.jsonl}
records \\texttt{cor:diag} verified against kernel 4.31.0 in one attempt,
and \\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-lake/mathlib\\_migration\\_receipts.jsonl}
records it verified against Mathlib in two attempts with axioms
\\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice}, \\texttt{Quot.sound}. A
surviving mutant that is not an equivalent mutant is therefore not
merely a test-suite gap: by this corollary it is evidence the staged
validator's invariant set does not fully capture the law it is meant to
enforce.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 4: The Agent8 Byte and Branchless Fleet Admission
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch4 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 4 ;
    doc:label "ch:agent8" ;
    doc:title "The Agent8 Byte and Branchless Fleet Admission" .

:ch4_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch4 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "The status byte as an OK-polarity projection" .

:ch4_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The eight-category refusal taxonomy that runs through this
whole thesis series has an immediate machine-word encoding once it is
projected to its OK polarity rather than its denial polarity.
\\begin{construction}[Agent8 byte]\\label{con:agent8}
Project each agent $a$ to a status byte $\\abyte(a)\\in\\{0,1\\}^8$ whose
lanes are the OK-polarity complements of the denial lanes (admitted,
evidence, budget, authority, healthy, conformant, receipted, replayable);
$\\mathbf1=\\code{0xFF}$ is the all-OK byte; an agent is admissible iff
$\\abyte(a)=\\mathbf1$, equivalently iff its 8-lane denial restriction is
$\\bm0$.
\\end{construction}
The corpus records this construction under the shared label
\\texttt{con:agent8} in both \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}'s
and \\texttt{projection\\_thesis.tex}'s RDF -- two distinct statements
with one label, disambiguated in \\texttt{docs/thesis/rdf/corpus.ttl} by
\\texttt{math:fromPaper}: this chapter's phrasing above, with the packed
$\\abyte$/\\code{0xFF} notation, is \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}'s
statement, while \\texttt{projection\\_thesis.tex}'s sibling statement
under the same label (``status byte $b\\in\\{0,1\\}^8$ whose lanes name
admitted, evidence-ok, within-budget, authority-bound, healthy,
conformant, receipted, replayable'') is the \\emph{same OK polarity}
stated without the packed-byte notation -- its lane names are the OK
conditions themselves (``evidence-ok'', ``healthy''), not their denials
-- so the two \\texttt{con:agent8} statements are notational variants of
one polarity, not an opposite-polarity pair. The two Lean files that
back the \\texttt{con:agent8} receipts disagree with \\emph{each other}
in polarity, not with either source statement:
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-pilot/con\\_agent8.lean} formalizes the
OK-polarity phrasing verbatim -- its \\texttt{byteAdmitted} is an
\\code{\\&\\&}-fold of eight named \\code{Bool} fields (\\code{admitted},
\\code{evidenceOk}, \\ldots, \\code{replayable}) that is \\code{true}
exactly when every lane holds, i.e.\\ the packed-byte Construction stated
above with no $\\abyte$/\\code{0xFF} numeral notation, so this file
\\emph{is} an independent kernel check of the OK-polarity Construction.
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-lake/Praxis/Corpus/con\\_agent8.lean}
instead reuses \\texttt{con:denial}'s \\code{Deny 8} type (a lane bit is
\\code{true} for a \\emph{denied} lane, \\code{⊥} the all-false
clean/admitted byte, per that file's own \\texttt{laneMap}) and defines
\\texttt{wordAdmits8 f} as the proposition \\code{wordOf8 f = 0\\#64}:
this is the denial-polarity dual of Corollary~\\ref{cor:onedenial}
below, not the OK-polarity phrasing shared by both source papers. Both
polarities of \\texttt{con:agent8} are therefore independently
kernel-checked, one per Lean file.
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-pilot/formalization\\_receipts.jsonl}
records \\texttt{con:agent8} verified (kernel 4.31.0, one attempt);
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-lake/mathlib\\_migration\\_receipts.jsonl}
records it verified in the Mathlib lane in three attempts with no axioms
used beyond the kernel's own.""" .

:ch4_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch4 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Bit-plane layouts and the branchless admission word" .

:ch4_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The agent8 byte of Construction~\\ref{con:agent8} can be
laid out two ways in memory, and the layout choice is what determines
whether fleet admission is a per-agent loop or a handful of word-parallel
instructions.
\\begin{definition}[AoS/SoA layouts]\\label{def:layouts}
The array-of-structures (AoS) layout stores each agent's 8-bit byte
contiguously, a 64-bit word holding 8 agents' bytes; the
structure-of-arrays (SoA / bit-plane) layout stores each lane $\\ell$ as
its own bitset $P_\\ell\\in\\{0,1\\}^N$, a 64-bit word of $P_\\ell$ holding
the lane-$\\ell$ bit of 64 agents.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{theorem}[Branchless admission]\\label{thm:branchless}
Under the SoA layout, the admissibility of 64 agents is computed by the
single branchless word expression $\\mathsf{adm}=P_1\\&\\cdots\\&P_8$; bit
$j$ of $\\mathsf{adm}$ is 1 iff agent $j$ has all eight lanes OK; the
computation uses exactly 7 AND instructions, no data-dependent branch,
and processes 64 agents, cost $7/64\\approx0.11$ instructions per agent.
\\end{theorem}""" .

:ch4_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch4 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "The denial-polarity dual" .

:ch4_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{corollary}[One-denial dual]\\label{cor:onedenial}
In denial polarity, with $D_\\ell=\\lnot P_\\ell$ the 64-agent word of
denial lane $\\ell$, the denied set is $\\bigvee_\\ell D_\\ell$ and the
admitted set its complement; fleet admission is the word-parallel join
of denial lanes followed by one complement, and adding a ninth lane
costs one more AND (resp. OR) per 64 agents.
\\end{corollary}
Theorem~\\ref{thm:branchless} and Corollary~\\ref{cor:onedenial} are the
same construction read in opposite polarity; extending the taxonomy
beyond eight lanes -- something the doctrine caps deliberately, per
Chapter~\\ref{ch:geometry}'s eight-member \\texttt{FailureClass} -- costs
exactly one more instruction per 64 agents in either reading, not a
redesign.""" .

:ch4_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch4 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:title "Borrow-safe SWAR zero-lane detection" .

:ch4_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{construction}[SWAR zero-lane mask]\\label{con:swar}
For a denial word $w\\in\\{0,1\\}^{64}$ of eight 8-bit lanes with
$L_{\\text{high}}=\\code{0x8080808080808080}$ and
$L_{\\text{low7}}=\\code{0x7f7f7f7f7f7f7f7f}$, the zero-lane bitmask is
$z(w)=\\lnot(((w\\&L_{\\text{low7}})+L_{\\text{low7}})\\lor w)\\&L_{\\text{high}}$;
bit 7 of lane $j$ in $z(w)$ is 1 iff lane $j$ in $w$ is exactly
\\code{0x00}.
\\end{construction}
This is the SWAR (SIMD-within-a-register) idiom for finding zero bytes
without a branch per lane and without leaking a borrow across lane
boundaries; it is kernel-verified in both lanes (bare-core: one attempt;
Mathlib: one attempt, no additional axioms), per the same two receipts
files cited above under \\texttt{con:swar}.""" .

:ch4_s4 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch4 ;
    doc:order 4 ;
    doc:title "Popcount admission in one instruction" .

:ch4_s4_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s4 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{theorem}[SWAR-verified sweep count]\\label{thm:swar-verify}
For a 64-bit word $w$ packing 8 agents gated against the broadcast
required mask, the number of admitted agents is
$\\text{admitted}(w)=\\code{popcount}(z(w))$, sweeping 8 agents in one CPU
instruction with zero branches and no lane borrow leakage.
\\end{theorem}
Kernel-verified in both lanes: bare-core one attempt (kernel 4.31.0),
Mathlib one attempt with axioms \\texttt{propext},
\\texttt{Classical.choice}, \\texttt{Quot.sound}, per
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-pilot/formalization\\_receipts.jsonl}
and \\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-lake/mathlib\\_migration\\_receipts.jsonl},
label \\texttt{thm:swar-verify}.""" .

:ch4_s5 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch4 ;
    doc:order 5 ;
    doc:title "Memory footprint: an exact identity" .

:ch4_s5_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s5 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{proposition}[Footprint identity]\\label{prop:footprint}
A population of $N=10^{10}$ agents, each an 8-bit byte, occupies
$N\\times8$ bits $=N$ bytes $=10^{10}$ bytes $=10\\,\\mathrm{GB}$ in either
layout; this is an exact arithmetic identity, not an estimate.
\\end{proposition}""" .

:ch4_s6 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch4 ;
    doc:order 6 ;
    doc:title "Sweep time: an order-of-magnitude estimate" .

:ch4_s6_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s6 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\textbf{Speculative.} The instruction count below is exact
arithmetic given the constructions above; the wall-clock time it takes
on any particular machine is not measured here and depends on memory
bandwidth this document does not benchmark.
\\begin{estimate}[Sweep cost]\\label{est:sweep}
A full admission sweep reads all 8 planes once ($10\\,\\mathrm{GB}$) and
performs $7N/64\\approx1.1\\times10^{9}$ AND instructions for
$N=10^{10}$; at streaming bandwidth
$\\Bw\\sim10^2$--$4\\times10^2\\,\\mathrm{GB/s}$,
$t_{\\text{sweep}}\\approx10\\,\\mathrm{GB}/\\Bw\\approx25$--$100\\,\\mathrm{ms}$;
the instruction count is exact, the wall-time is an order-of-magnitude
estimate.
\\end{estimate}""" .

:ch4_s6_p1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch4_s6 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\textbf{Speculative}, inheriting Estimate~\\ref{est:sweep}'s
bandwidth assumption.
\\begin{corollary}[Throughput]\\label{cor:throughput}
A single commodity server admits $N=10^{10}$ agents in
$\\sim25$--$100\\,\\mathrm{ms}$, i.e. at $\\sim10^{11}$--$4\\times10^{11}$
agent-admission decisions per second per server, bandwidth-bound.
\\end{corollary}""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 5: Planetary Economics of Admission (Speculative)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch5 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 5 ;
    doc:label "ch:planetary" ;
    doc:title "Planetary Economics of Admission (Speculative)" .

:ch5_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inChapter :ch5 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\textbf{This entire chapter is speculative.} Every
quantity in it is a Fermi estimate -- an order-of-magnitude
extrapolation to a planetary population of agents this document has not
built, measured, or deployed. Nothing here is derived from the
kernel-verified constructions of Chapter~\\ref{ch:agent8}; it is
motivated by them. No claim in this chapter should be read as evidence
for anything outside it.""" .

:ch5_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch5 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "Demand" .

:ch5_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch5_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{speculative}[Admission demand]\\label{est:demand}
For a population $N\\in[10^9,10^{12}]$ agents each re-admitted at rate
$\\rho\\in[1,10^2]\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, aggregate admission demand is
$\\Lambda_{\\text{demand}}=N\\rho\\in[10^9,10^{14}]$ decisions/s, central
estimate $\\sim10^{11}$--$10^{12}$ decisions/s for $N\\sim10^{10}$,
$\\rho\\sim10$.
\\end{speculative}""" .

:ch5_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch5 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Comprehension-based supply ceiling" .

:ch5_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch5_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{speculative}[Comprehension-supply ceiling]\\label{est:comp-supply}
A comprehension-based admission decision costs $\\sim10^{-2}$--$10^0\\,\\mathrm{s}$
of accelerator time, i.e. $\\sim1$--$10^2$ decisions/s per accelerator; a
planetary fleet of $\\sim10^6$--$10^7$ accelerators yields a
comprehension-supply ceiling $\\Lambda_{\\text{comp}}\\lesssim10^7$--$10^9$
decisions/s, central estimate $\\sim10^8$ decisions/s.
\\end{speculative}""" .

:ch5_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch5 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "Bit-parallel supply" .

:ch5_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch5_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{speculative}[Bit-parallel supply]\\label{est:bit-supply}
One commodity server supplies $\\sim10^{11}$ decisions/s bandwidth-bound;
a rack of $\\sim10^2$ servers supplies $\\sim10^{13}$ decisions/s,
$\\sim10^7\\times$ cheaper per decision than an LLM judgment, so
bit-parallel supply scales into the demand band on a single facility.
\\end{speculative}""" .

:ch5_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch5 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:title "The affordability separation theorem" .

:ch5_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch5_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{speculative}[Affordability separation]\\label{thm:afford}
For demand $\\Lambda_{\\text{demand}}$ and supplies
$\\Lambda_{\\text{comp}},\\Lambda_{\\text{bit}}$:
$\\Lambda_{\\text{comp}}\\sim10^8\\ll\\Lambda_{\\text{demand}}\\sim10^{11}$--$10^{12}\\lesssim\\Lambda_{\\text{bit}}\\sim10^{11}$--$10^{13}$;
comprehension-based admission is under-provisioned by three to four
orders of magnitude for a $10^{10}$-agent fleet, while bit-parallel
admission meets demand on a single facility, and the separation persists
under an order-of-magnitude perturbation of any single input.
\\end{speculative}
\\begin{speculative}[Consequence]\\label{cor:onlybit}
A planetary control plane that re-admits its population continuously
cannot be built on comprehension: no attainable accelerator fleet closes
the demand gap; it can be built on the sweep, whose cost is dominated by
a single pass over $10\\,\\mathrm{GB}$ of memory; comprehension is
reserved for sampled audit at honest $O(n)$ replay cost.
\\end{speculative}
Despite being labelled ``theorem'' and ``corollary'' in the source
material's own environment names, both statements are Fermi-estimate
consequences chained from three order-of-magnitude inputs
(Estimates~\\ref{est:demand}--\\ref{est:bit-supply}); this document keeps
them in the \\textsc{speculative} register and environment throughout,
naming the mismatch rather than inheriting it silently.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 6: The Antibody Clause
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch6 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 6 ;
    doc:label "ch:antibody" ;
    doc:title "The Antibody Clause: Claim Magnitude Bounded by Receipting" .

:ch6_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch6 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "Claims and receipt witnesses" .

:ch6_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch6_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Chapter~\\ref{ch:planetary} was explicit that its numbers
are not receipted. This chapter states the general mechanism that makes
that distinction mechanical rather than a matter of authorial honesty:
a claim's admissible magnitude is bounded by what its receipt witness
can attest, full stop.
\\begin{definition}[Claim and witness]\\label{def:claim}
A claim is a pair $c=(\\phi_c,w_c)$ where $\\phi_c$ is a proposition and
$w_c\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge0}$ its asserted magnitude; a receipt witness for
$c$ is a receipt $r(c)$ whose boundary projection is accepted,
$V(\\Proj(r(c)))=1$, and whose committed fields entail an attested
magnitude $\\widehat w_c$; a claim is admissible iff it has a receipt
witness with $w_c\\le\\widehat w_c$.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{definition}[Overclaim set, antibody invariant]\\label{def:over}
The overclaim set of a system emitting claims $\\mathcal C$ is
$\\Over=\\{c\\in\\mathcal C:\\nexists\\,r(c)\\text{ with }V(\\Proj(r(c)))=1\\text{ and }w_c\\le\\widehat w_c\\}$;
the antibody invariant is $\\Over=\\varnothing$, the
\\code{docs-exceed-mechanism} defect class enforced as a gate.
\\end{definition}
Note: \\texttt{docs-exceed-mechanism} is the corpus's own name, quoted
verbatim above from \\texttt{docs/thesis/rdf/04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.ttl}'s
\\texttt{def:over}, for the conceptual defect class this invariant guards
against. It names no compiled gate, lint, or CI check in this repository:
\\texttt{grep -rn "docs-exceed-mechanism" crates/ src/} returns zero
matches, and no \\texttt{.rq} or \\texttt{.sparql} file exists anywhere in
the repository. The paper's own antibody-clause check, discussed below in
this chapter, is likewise a proposed SPARQL query, not an implemented
one.""" .

:ch6_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch6 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "The Magnitude Theorem" .

:ch6_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch6_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{theorem}[Magnitude]\\label{thm:magnitude}
If a system maintains $\\Over=\\varnothing$, then for every emitted claim
$c$, $w_c\\le\\widehat w_c=\\max\\{w:\\text{the committed fields of }r(c)\\text{ entail magnitude }w\\}$,
verifiable at boundary cost $O(\\CL)$ independent of the interior that
produced $c$; no claim can exceed what its mechanism can receipt.
\\end{theorem}""" .

:ch6_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch6 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "Reflexive application: this paper's own overclaim set" .

:ch6_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch6_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{corollary}[Reflexive application]\\label{cor:reflexive}
Each quantitative claim in this paper is either a theorem with a proof
or an Estimate explicitly labelled with its inputs; the partition is
exhaustive, so this paper's overclaim set is empty, and by the
magnitude theorem the magnitude of each claim is bounded by its witness
as the invariant requires.
\\end{corollary}
Note: this consolidated paper generalizes that theorem-or-Estimate
partition one level, to the \\texttt{doc:register} vocabulary
(\\texttt{packs/doc-pack/ontology.ttl}) that every paragraph in this file
actually carries. Each quantitative claim here carries one of two
\\texttt{doc:register} values: \\textsc{evidence-bound} -- a theorem,
proposition, or corollary with a proof, or a Measurement reporting
values observed from an executed run (Chapter~\\ref{ch:cell}) -- or
\\textsc{speculative} -- an Estimate, or a statement kept in the
\\texttt{speculative} environment and explicitly labelled with its
inputs, including Fermi chains that the source material itself calls
``theorem''/``corollary'' but that this document keeps under the
speculative register (Chapter~\\ref{ch:planetary}'s naming mismatch is
disclosed where it occurs rather than inherited silently). This
register partition is exhaustive over the \\texttt{theorem},
\\texttt{proposition}, \\texttt{corollary}, \\texttt{estimate},
\\texttt{speculative}, and \\texttt{measurement} environments actually
used in this document, so Corollary~\\ref{cor:reflexive}'s partition
still holds under this finer-grained labeling, and this paper's
overclaim set remains empty under either partition.
This document's own \\texttt{doc:register} property (evidence-bound /
speculative on every paragraph, per
\\texttt{packs/doc-pack/ontology.ttl}) would be the concrete, checkable
substrate for a SPARQL query over \\texttt{rdf/thesis.ttl} that finds
any paragraph with neither register value, or any speculative paragraph
in a chapter titled without the word ``speculative'' next to an
evidence-bound one -- exactly a check for $\\Over\\ne\\varnothing$ in the
antibody-clause sense above. No such query is implemented in this
repository today (Chapter~\\ref{ch:antibody}'s opening section notes the
same gap for \\code{docs-exceed-mechanism} generally); this paragraph
describes what the check would be, not a check that runs.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 7: Derived Supervision Topology
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch7 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 7 ;
    doc:label "ch:topology" ;
    doc:title "Derived Supervision Topology" .

:ch7_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch7 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "Stage depth from the data-dependency DAG" .

:ch7_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch7_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex} derives supervision
structure rather than accepting it as hand-authored, starting from the
plan's own data-dependency edges -- the same DAG that
\\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/dag.rs} builds and that the
solver's saturated fact base already commits to before any node runs.
\\begin{definition}[Stage depth]\\label{def:depth}
Let $\\Dag = (V, E)$ be the plan's data-dependency DAG (edge $u \\to v$
iff an add-effect of $u$ feeds a precondition of $v$); define $d(v) = 0$
for sources and $d(v) = 1 + \\max_{u \\to v} d(u)$ otherwise. A stage
$S_k = \\{v : d(v) = k\\}$.
\\end{definition}
Kernel-verified in both lanes (bare-core and Mathlib, label
\\texttt{def:depth}), per
\\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-pilot/formalization\\_receipts.jsonl}
and \\texttt{tools/paper-factory/lean-lake/mathlib\\_migration\\_receipts.jsonl}.""" .

:ch7_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch7 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Restart strategy earned from the dependent set" .

:ch7_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch7_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Every stage in Definition~\\ref{def:depth} needs a restart
policy when one of its members crashes, and \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}
derives which policy from the same dependency edges rather than letting
an author pick one -- implemented in
\\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/supervise.rs}.
\\begin{definition}[Earned restart strategy]\\label{def:earned}
Stage $S_k$ supervises \\textsc{RestForOne} iff some $v \\in S_k$ has a
nonempty transitive dependent set $D(v)$; otherwise \\textsc{OneForOne}.
The cohort of $v$ is $\\{v\\} \\cup D(v)$ under \\textsc{RestForOne} and
$\\{v\\}$ under \\textsc{OneForOne}.
\\end{definition}""" .

:ch7_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch7 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "The sealed derive constructor and topology\\_hash" .

:ch7_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch7_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{proposition}[Sealed topology]\\label{prop:topology}
$\\Topo = (\\mathrm{stages}, \\mathrm{policy})$ is produced only by
\\textsf{derive} (a sealed constructor: no literal construction
compiles), and
$\\mathrm{topology\\_hash} = \\ca(\\mathrm{stages}\\,\\Vert\\,\\mathrm{policy}\\,\\Vert\\,\\mathrm{plan\\_hash}\\,\\Vert\\,\\mathrm{problem\\_hash})$
joins the receipt lineage. Determinism is test-pinned: same plan, same
hash.
\\end{proposition}
There is no code path by which a human-authored supervision tree can
enter the system: the only constructor that yields a
\\texttt{SupervisionTopology} value is \\textsf{derive}, and its output is
folded into the receipt chain by \\texttt{topology\\_hash} before any node
executes.""" .

:ch7_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch7 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:title "Refusal: no OneForAll" .

:ch7_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch7_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{refusal}[No OneForAll]\\label{refusal:oneforall}
No \\textsc{OneForAll}: OTP justifies one-for-all by shared mutable fate
between siblings. In an acyclic data-flow plan, same-depth siblings are
independent by construction -- the coupling one-for-all encodes is
inexpressible. The variant does not appear in the Strategy enum at all;
an exhaustive-match test makes its future addition a compilation event,
forcing the doctrine conversation.
\\end{refusal}""" .

:ch7_s4 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch7 ;
    doc:order 4 ;
    doc:title "Refusal: no SimpleOneForOne, no clamped intensity" .

:ch7_s4_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch7_s4 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{refusal}[No SimpleOneForOne, no clamping]\\label{refusal:simpleoneforone}
No \\textsc{SimpleOneForOne}; intensity $> 8$ refused, not clamped:
derived plans have static step sets, and
\\texttt{RestartPolicy::new(9, \\_)} returns a Refusal -- the byte
governor applies to recovery itself, and silently clamping a stated
policy would be the executor editing the operator's law.
\\end{refusal}
Kernel-verified: bare-core, verified in one attempt (kernel 4.31.0);
Mathlib lane, verified in two attempts with axioms
\\texttt{RestartPolicy}, \\texttt{no\\_simple\\_one\\_for\\_one},
\\texttt{RestartPolicy.new} -- per the same two receipts files, label
\\texttt{refusal:simpleoneforone}.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 8: Derived Failure Geometry
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch8 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 8 ;
    doc:label "ch:geometry" ;
    doc:title "Derived Failure Geometry" .

:ch8_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch8 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "The eight-member FailureClass enum" .

:ch8_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch8_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """A supervision topology says who restarts with whom; it
does not say what a given failure was. \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}
closes that gap with a closed, eight-member failure taxonomy --
implemented in \\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/geometry.rs} -- capped
at eight by the same doctrine that bounds the agent8 taxonomy of
Chapter~\\ref{ch:agent8}.
\\begin{definition}[FailureClass]\\label{def:classes}
$\\mathsf{FailureClass} = \\{$\\textsf{LogicFault}, \\textsf{BudgetBreach},
\\textsf{AuthorityVacuum}, \\textsf{TransientFault}, \\textsf{Stall},
\\textsf{StarvedInput}, \\textsf{CertifiedUnsat},
\\textsf{GeometryGap}$\\}$ -- the semantic axis, capped at eight by the
doctrine.
\\end{definition}
Kernel-verified in both lanes: bare-core verified; Mathlib verified with
axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice}, \\texttt{Quot.sound},
label \\texttt{def:classes}.""" .

:ch8_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch8 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Branches and first-match classification" .

:ch8_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch8_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{definition}[Branch, geometry]\\label{def:branch}
A branch is $(c, \\Sigma, r)$: a class, a conjunction of signal
predicates over a crash snapshot, and a lawful response
$r \\in \\{\\textsf{Restart}, \\textsf{Park}(\\rho), \\textsf{Refuse}(\\mathrm{core}), \\textsf{Escalate}\\}$.
The geometry $\\Geo$ maps each node to an ordered branch list;
classification is first-match-wins;
$\\mathrm{geometry\\_hash} = \\ca(\\Geo\\,\\Vert\\,\\mathrm{topology\\_hash})$.
\\end{definition}
Chaining \\texttt{geometry\\_hash} to \\texttt{topology\\_hash}
(Proposition~\\ref{prop:topology}) means a failure geometry cannot be
attached to a plan after the fact without changing the receipt of the
plan it claims to cover.""" .

:ch8_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch8 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "Completeness by construction: the gap outcome" .

:ch8_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch8_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The eighth member of Definition~\\ref{def:classes},
\\textsf{GeometryGap}, is not an ordinary failure class: it is the
system's own admission that its geometry can be incomplete.
\\begin{theorem}[Geometry-gap completeness]\\label{thm:geomgap}
\\textsf{GeometryGap} is the value classification returns when no stored
branch matches; it is never a member of any stored list. Consequently no
branch ordering, addition, or deletion can capture, mask, or remove the
gap outcome: the map admits its own incompleteness by construction, not
by convention.
\\end{theorem}
This is the same guarantee, at the failure-classification layer, that
\\textsf{Rfsl} gives at the admission layer in the keystone equation: an
outcome that names ``I don't have a rule for this'' rather than silently
defaulting. The corpus records this theorem under the shared label
\\texttt{thm:gap} in both \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}'s and
\\texttt{projection\\_thesis.tex}'s RDF -- two distinct statements with
one label, disambiguated in \\texttt{docs/thesis/rdf/corpus.ttl} by
\\texttt{math:fromPaper}; to avoid that collision this document uses its
own label, \\texttt{thm:geomgap}, for the statement above.""" .

:ch8_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch8 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:title "Fragile preconditions" .

:ch8_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch8_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Not every recoverable-looking fact is actually
recoverable; the geometry needs a name for the ones that are not.
\\begin{definition}[Fragile precondition]\\label{def:fragile}
A precondition predicate $p$ of capability $c$ is fragile iff no
capability in the problem produces $p$. The initial state is a one-time
gift: if a fragile fact is lost mid-run, nothing in the plan can
lawfully re-produce it -- whereas a fact with even one producer is
recoverable by restarting that producer.
\\end{definition}""" .

:ch8_s4 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch8 ;
    doc:order 4 ;
    doc:title "Total accounting" .

:ch8_s4_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch8_s4 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{proposition}[Total accounting]\\label{prop:totalaccounting}
Every node of every supervised run carries exactly one disposition
(\\textsf{Completed}$(r)$, \\textsf{Parked}, \\textsf{SkippedBy},
\\textsf{GaveUp}); no silent rows exist (test-pinned:
$|\\mathrm{dispositions}| = |V|$).
\\end{proposition}
Together, Theorem~\\ref{thm:geomgap} and Proposition~\\ref{prop:totalaccounting}
are the failure-geometry analogue of the antibody clause
(Chapter~\\ref{ch:antibody}): every node has a named outcome, and any
outcome the geometry cannot name surfaces as \\textsf{GeometryGap} rather
than disappearing.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 9: Supervised Execution -- Cell Measurements
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch9 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 9 ;
    doc:label "ch:cell" ;
    doc:title "Supervised Execution: Cell Measurements" .

:ch9_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch9 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "Recovery is invisible in the receipt chain" .

:ch9_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch9_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Chapters~\\ref{ch:topology}--\\ref{ch:geometry} derive
structure before any node runs; this chapter is the paper's own primary
empirical contribution, reporting what happens when that structure is
exercised by actually-injected faults.
\\begin{measurement}[Recovery is invisible]\\label{meas:recoveryinvisible}
On the lawobject plan with two injected transient crashes at judge: both
crashes land in named branches (TransientFault, then Stall), the run
completes, and the final root hash is byte-identical to the crash-free
run's. Park-then-re-admit heals to the same identity; machine death
composes: kill-9 mid-restart-loop, WAL recovery, identical receipt.
\\end{measurement}
Kernel-verified in both lanes (label \\texttt{meas:recoveryinvisible}),
bare-core in one attempt, Mathlib in one attempt with axiom
\\texttt{meas\\_recoveryinvisible.recoveryinvisible}.""" .

:ch9_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch9 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "The 10,000-member cell" .

:ch9_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch9_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{measurement}[10,000-member cell]\\label{meas:cell}
The supervised cell, 10,000 members, release: overhead of supervision at
fault rate 0 is $-0.9\\%$ of medians over five paired runs, inside the
baseline's own $\\pm 5.7\\%$ run spread -- statistical noise. Recovery
counts track injected transient rates exactly: $69 / 608 / 3{,}179$
recovered at $1\\% / 10\\% / 50\\%$. Throughput is flat across all fault
rates. The crash-looping template is detected at quorum and quarantined
by epoch 2 in every configuration.
\\end{measurement}
Kernel-verified in both lanes, label \\texttt{meas:cell}: bare-core one
attempt, Mathlib two attempts with axiom
\\texttt{CellMeasurement.overhead\\_within\\_baseline\\_spread} (recorded
as not depending on any further axioms). Implemented in
\\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/cell\\_supervise.rs} and
\\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/fault.rs}.""" .

:ch9_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch9 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "What flat throughput means" .

:ch9_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch9_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{remark}[Flat throughput]\\label{remark:flatthroughput}
What flat throughput means: the cost of a supervised fleet tracked its
failure novelty, not its failure rate: recovered transients are absorbed
at boundary cost, and the one genuinely novel pathology (the
crash-looping class) was converted --- once, by quorum --- into a cheap
standing refusal. The full novelty-curve-under-faults re-measurement is
receipted as deferred, not claimed.
\\end{remark}""" .

:ch9_s3 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch9 ;
    doc:order 3 ;
    doc:title "The deferred novelty-curve measurement" .

:ch9_s3_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch9_s3 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The remark above names a measurement this paper does not
have; the following refusal is what keeps that admission from being a
silent omission.
\\begin{refusal}[No novelty-curve measurement yet]\\label{ref:curve}
No novelty-curve-under-faults measurement -- yet: member-level fault
injection recovers without re-running solver work, so a work-proxy
re-measurement would overstate the cache dividend -- the flattering
error. The claim is withheld until node-level injection is wired through
the fleet path; the receipt file names this in its own deferred list.
\\end{refusal}
This refusal is itself kernel-verified (label \\texttt{ref:curve}):
bare-core two attempts; Mathlib one attempt, with axioms
\\texttt{Praxis.Corpus.RefCurve.NoveltyCurveUnderFaultsWithheld} and
\\texttt{Praxis.Corpus.RefCurve.noveltyCurveUnderFaultsWithheld} explicitly
documented as project-specific epistemic markers with no Mathlib
equivalent -- a formalization of ``we know we have not measured this,''
not of the measurement itself.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 10: Fleet Application Tooling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch10 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 10 ;
    doc:label "ch:fleettool" ;
    doc:title "Fleet Application Tooling: Retrofit and Reconciliation" .

:ch10_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inChapter :ch10 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The oracle discipline of Chapter~\\ref{ch:oracle} and the
supervised-execution discipline of Chapters~\\ref{ch:topology}--\\ref{ch:cell}
both need a way to apply a change to a running fleet without ever
letting a failing change reach the fleet's shared branch, and a way to
measure what to change in the first place. That pair of mechanisms --
an AST-gated, Git-worktree-isolated applier (worktree creation, the
change commit, and the push back to origin implemented in
\\texttt{crates/praxis-retrofit/src/fleet\\_apply.rs}; the CI-gate
battery and the unconditional rollback-on-failure protocol implemented
in the sibling file \\texttt{crates/praxis-retrofit/src/fleet\\_validate.rs})
and the residual-vector/gap-report reconciliation apparatus that names
the applier's target -- is this chapter's subject, matching the two
halves of its own title, retrofit and reconciliation; it is
fleet-application tooling, not an oracle-trust argument, and is placed
here rather than beside Chapter~\\ref{ch:oracle} for that reason. Two
disclosures on how \\texttt{fleet\\_validate.rs} relates to the definitions
below, checked directly against that file: first, its
\\texttt{capture\\_git\\_state}/\\texttt{restore\\_git\\_state} functions and
\\texttt{GitState} struct back the isolation-and-unconditional-rollback
mechanism of Definition~\\ref{def:worktree}, Definition~\\ref{def:worktree-ret},
and Proposition~\\ref{prop:mainclean} -- the struct's field is literally
named \\texttt{commit\\_sha}, which is why the three definitions below
render the rollback target as \\code{commit\\_sha} rather than the
corpus's \\code{baseline\\_sha} token (\\texttt{docs/thesis/rdf/04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.ttl}'s
\\texttt{def:worktree}, \\texttt{def:worktree-ret}, and \\texttt{prop:mainclean}
all use \\code{baseline\\_sha}; this is the one token substitution made
against the verbatim-quotation rule of Chapter~\\ref{ch:intro}, disclosed
here rather than three times). Second, the file's \\texttt{CiGateName} enum
does \\emph{not} back the three-gate CI oracle battery
(\\code{cargo build}, \\code{cargo test}, \\code{cargo clippy}) that
Definition~\\ref{def:worktree-ret} quotes from the corpus: the actual enum
has five variants (\\texttt{Fmt}, \\texttt{Clippy}, \\texttt{Test},
\\texttt{Deny}, \\texttt{Typos}), running \\texttt{cargo fmt --check},
\\texttt{cargo clippy}, \\texttt{cargo test}, \\texttt{cargo deny check},
and a \\texttt{typos} check respectively, with no build gate at all.
Definition~\\ref{def:worktree-ret}'s gate list is quoted verbatim from the
corpus and is left unchanged below; it names the CI battery as the
04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex corpus statement described it at the time,
not as \\texttt{fleet\\_validate.rs}'s \\texttt{CiGateName} enum stands
today. Every labeled
definition and proposition below is, in the source-attribution sense of
Chapter~\\ref{ch:intro}, drawn from \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}
rather than \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}, even though this chapter
sits among the chapters that otherwise instantiate the latter:
\\texttt{def:astgate}, \\texttt{prop:astterm}, \\texttt{def:worktree},
\\texttt{def:worktree-ret}, \\texttt{prop:mainclean},
\\texttt{def:residual}, \\texttt{def:vizgap}, and \\texttt{prop:vizgap}
are all \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex} statements per
\\texttt{docs/thesis/rdf/corpus.ttl}'s \\texttt{math:fromPaper}; this
chapter is placed here because its subject (fleet application tooling)
follows naturally after the supervised-execution material, not because
its source paper does.""" .

:ch10_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch10 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "AST gates and the isolated worktree applier" .

:ch10_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch10_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{definition}[AST gate]\\label{def:astgate}
An AST gate is a syntactic predicate $g:\\mathcal T\\to\\{0,1\\}$ on an
abstract syntax tree $\\mathcal T$, computable in $O(|\\mathcal T|)$ time;
the retrofit applier gates every proposed change through an ordered
battery $(g_1,\\dots,g_m)$: the change is admitted iff all gates pass,
$\\bigwedge_i g_i(\\mathcal T)=1$; a failing gate returns a denial with the
gate index and offending AST node.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{proposition}[Battery termination]\\label{prop:astterm}
For any finite AST $\\mathcal T$ and finite gate battery
$(g_1,\\dots,g_m)$ of syntactic predicates, battery evaluation terminates
in $O(m\\cdot|\\mathcal T|)$ time and returns either \\code{Admitted} or a
refusal carrying the first failing gate index and offending node; the
retraction is total, never diverging and never returning an unlabelled
outcome.
\\end{proposition}""" .

:ch10_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch10 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Git-worktree isolation end to end" .

:ch10_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch10_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The gate battery of Definition~\\ref{def:astgate} decides
admission of a change; isolating the attempt to apply it from the
fleet's shared branch is a second, orthogonal mechanism.
\\begin{definition}[Worktree isolation]\\label{def:worktree}
An applier isolates modifications by spawning a decoupled working tree
via \\code{git worktree add temp\\_path phase\\_branch}, running
validation gates and executing the auto-rollback protocol
\\code{git reset --hard commit\\_sha} on failure to guarantee main
branch cleanliness.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{definition}[Worktree-isolated CI application]\\label{def:worktree-ret}
The retrofit applier isolates each application attempt in a fresh Git
worktree, applies the change, runs the CI oracle $(g_1,\\dots,g_m)$
(\\code{cargo build}, \\code{cargo test}, \\code{cargo clippy}), and on any
gate failure executes \\code{git reset --hard commit\\_sha}; on
all-pass the worktree change is merged and the chain receipt for the
application is minted with the worktree OID committed into the frame's
\\code{obj\\_refs} payload digest.
\\end{definition}""" .

:ch10_s1_p1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch10_s1 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{proposition}[Main stays clean]\\label{prop:mainclean}
Under the isolated worktree applier, the main branch is never advanced
past a failing CI gate; the rollback protocol is unconditional on
failure, running before the worktree is removed, restoring the branch to
\\code{commit\\_sha} regardless of which gate failed; hence the main
branch satisfies all CI gates at every point in its history.
\\end{proposition}
Definitions~\\ref{def:worktree} and~\\ref{def:worktree-ret} name the same
isolation mechanism at two levels of detail -- the first as the
coarse-grained mechanism statement, the second the more operational,
CI-oracle-specific form -- and both are \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}
statements (per \\texttt{docs/thesis/rdf/corpus.ttl}'s
\\texttt{math:fromPaper}; \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex} has no worktree
material at all). Proposition~\\ref{prop:mainclean}
is the guarantee both are stated to establish: the operator's global
\\texttt{/Users/sac/CLAUDE.md} fix-forward-only invariant (no destructive
git operations on the shared history) -- distinct from this repository's
own \\texttt{CLAUDE.md}, which carries a different, narrower set of
invariants (Chapter~\\ref{ch:scope} below cites one) -- is exactly
what an unconditional, gate-failure-triggered rollback of an isolated
worktree is built to enforce mechanically.""" .

:ch10_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch10 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "Autonomic reconciliation: residual vectors and gap reports" .

:ch10_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch10_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """A gated, isolated applier still needs to know what to
change; autonomic reconciliation is the measurement step that produces
that target, and it obeys the same $O(\\CL)$ boundary-cost discipline as
the receipt projection of Chapter~\\ref{ch:gap}.
\\begin{definition}[Residual vector]\\label{def:residual}
For environmental observations $O$ and target artifact $A$, the
measurement $\\muop(O)$ returns a residual vector $R\\in\\mathbb{R}^k$ with
$r_i=\\text{measured}_i-\\text{midpoint}(\\text{target}_i)$; reconciliation
selects the dominant dimension $\\arg\\max_i|r_i|$ and applies a repair
operator subject to \\code{RepairBand} limits.
\\end{definition}
Note: \\code{RepairBand} is the corpus's own name for this limit, quoted
verbatim above; it is not a named type in this codebase today. The
sketch at \\texttt{crates/praxis-reconciler/src/loop\\_logic.rs} is not a
Cargo workspace member (absent from the \\texttt{[workspace] members}
list in \\texttt{Cargo.toml}) and imports its operator/report types from
a \\texttt{genesis\\_types\\_v2} crate that does not exist anywhere in
this repository, so no \\code{RepairBand} or other bound-enforcing type
is compiled or checkable today; the definition above states the design
intent, not a verified property of running code.
\\begin{definition}[Visual gap report]\\label{def:vizgap}
A visual gap report is the output of \\code{measure\\_gap}: for each
reconcilable dimension $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,k\\}$, the residual
$r_i=\\text{measured}_i-\\text{midpoint}(\\text{target}_i)$ and the
dominant dimension $i^\\star=\\argmax_i|r_i|$ together with a rendered
diff block; the report has $k$ residual values and one dominant index,
size $O(k)$ independent of the interior.
\\end{definition}""" .

:ch10_s2_p1 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch10_s2 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{proposition}[Gap report cost]\\label{prop:vizgap}
The visual gap report gives $\\mathrm{gap}:\\mathbb R^k\\to\\mathbb R^k\\times[k]$,
a projection with the same cost structure as the receipt projection,
$O(k)$ to compute and $O(k)$ to verify; the repair operator acts on the
dominant dimension $i^\\star$ subject to \\code{RepairBand} bounds,
producing at most one corrective actuation, a bounded deterministic
manufacture.
\\end{proposition}
Note: as Definition~\\ref{def:residual}'s note above discloses,
\\code{RepairBand} is not yet a compiled, named bound type in this
repository, so the bound above is the corpus's stated design intent, not
a verified property of running code.
Reconciliation therefore never inspects more than $O(k)$ reconcilable
dimensions to decide its one corrective action, the same shape of
argument Chapter~\\ref{ch:gap} makes for receipt verification at
$O(\\CL)$: a bounded-size projection stands in for an unbounded
interior.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 11: Fable Harness
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch11 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 11 ;
    doc:label "ch:fable" ;
    doc:title "Fable Harness: Spec-Driven LLM Evaluation" .

:ch11_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch11 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:label "sec:fable-mechanics" ;
    doc:title "Harness mechanics" .

:ch11_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch11_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The oracle discipline of Chapter~\\ref{ch:oracle} applies
directly to evaluating whether a language-model-generated implementation
is correct; \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}'s Fable harness
(\\texttt{crates/rust-fable-testbed}, \\texttt{docs/fable/}) is that
oracle instantiated for LLM-generated code, run hermetically against a
mock model membrane so the verdict never depends on a live API call.
\\begin{definition}[Fable harness]\\label{def:fable}
A Fable harness is a test environment that constructs a prompt $P_T$
from a task ontology $T$, calls a mock model membrane $M$ that
intercepts the LLM API and returns a deterministic response drawn from a
test fixture indexed by $P_T$'s hash, extracts the code block $C$ from
the model response $R$, and runs the verification oracle
$\\mathrm{Oracle}(T,R)$ against $C$ in an isolated cargo workspace. The
mock membrane ensures that harness runs are hermetic: no real LLM call
is made, the response is deterministic given $P_T$, and the oracle
verdict is reproducible.
\\end{definition}
\\begin{definition}[Sandbox oracle]\\label{def:sandbox}
Let $P_T$ be a prompt compiled from task ontology $T$, and let $C$ be
the code block extracted from model response $R$. The sandbox
verification oracle is the composite:
$\\text{Oracle}(T, R) = \\text{cargo\\_build}(C) \\land \\text{cargo\\_test}(C) \\land \\text{cargo\\_clippy}(C) \\land \\text{safety\\_audit}(C)$.
The outcome is receipted and chained to the ledger using rolling BLAKE3
hashes \\cite{blake3}.
\\end{definition}
Both are kernel-verified in both lanes (\\texttt{def:fable},
\\texttt{def:sandbox}); the Mathlib-lane receipt for \\texttt{def:sandbox}
names five project-specific axioms
(\\texttt{Praxis.Corpus.DefSandbox.\\{blake3Chain,cargoBuild,cargoClippy,cargoTest,safetyAudit\\}})
rather than proving the four external tools' behavior from first
principles -- the formalization is of the composite's shape, not a
re-verification of \\texttt{cargo} itself.""" .

:ch11_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch11 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Receipting fable runs" .

:ch11_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch11_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{construction}[Fable receipt chain]\\label{con:fablechain}
When $\\mathrm{Oracle}(T,R)=1$, the harness mints a receipt using the
standard \\code{OcelCausalFrame} chain:
$h_{+} = \\chainH(h_{-}\\,\\Vert\\,\\mathrm{body}(\\fr))$, where the frame's
\\code{obj\\_refs} carry $\\dg(C)=\\chainH(\\text{canonical bytes of }C)$.
The genesis seed is $\\chainH(\\text{``fable-v1-genesis''})$,
domain-isolated from the plan chain so fable receipts cannot
cross-verify with plan receipts even if their terminal hashes collide.
\\end{construction}
Kernel-verified in both lanes, label \\texttt{con:fablechain}: bare-core
one attempt; Mathlib two attempts with axioms \\texttt{chainH},
\\texttt{propext}.""" .

:ch11_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch11 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:label "sec:fable-speculative" ;
    doc:title "Speculative: decidability and differential-oracle claims" .

:ch11_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch11_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "speculative" ;
    doc:body """\\textbf{Speculative, and flagged as under-supported.} The
harness mechanics above (Definitions~\\ref{def:fable},~\\ref{def:sandbox},
Construction~\\ref{con:fablechain}) are evidence-bound: they describe
what the harness does and how it receipts. Whether
$\\mathrm{Oracle}(T,R)$ is a decidable oracle in the sense of
Definition~\\ref{def:oracle} is a separate, stronger claim, and the
corpus RDF for \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex} itself marks both proof
steps of that claim \\textsc{UNRESOLVED}: in \\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}'s
own, standalone RDF they lean on an external paper's (``Paper~04'')
Definition~4.7 and Proposition~4.4. Unlike in that standalone source,
those two statements are not external to \\emph{this} document: Paper~04
is \\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}, which this consolidation
folds in as Chapters~\\ref{ch:gap}--\\ref{ch:antibody} and~\\ref{ch:fleettool}.
\\texttt{synthesis\\_thesis.tex}'s own ``Definition 4.7''/``Proposition
4.4'' numbering does not resolve against this paper's actual
chapter/theorem counters (\\texttt{04\\_projection\\_and\\_scale.tex}'s
own Chapter~4 has one definition and a Proposition~4.4 of its own,
Proposition~\\ref{prop:footprint}, neither of which is the statement
being cited), so the two citations are matched here by \\emph{content},
not by number: the staged validator that step cites is
Definition~\\ref{def:staged}, and the ``genuine defect, not a property
of the input'' soundness result that step cites is
Proposition~\\ref{prop:fuzz}, both of Chapter~\\ref{ch:oracle} above,
both already stated and kernel-checked in this corpus. That resolves
the \\emph{availability}
gap but not the \\emph{proof} gap: Proposition~\\ref{prop:fuzz}'s
``genuine defect, not a property of the input'' argument is proved for
the admission retraction $\\adm$, and the Mutant Kill Theorem
(Theorem~\\ref{thm:kill}) needs every stage of a staged validator to be
individually sound \\emph{and} complete (Definition~\\ref{def:staged}) to
license the analogous claim for $\\mathrm{Oracle}(T,R)$'s four stages
(\\code{cargo\\_build}, \\code{cargo\\_test}, \\code{cargo\\_clippy},
\\code{safety\\_audit}); no statement in this corpus proves that those
four specific stages are each sound and complete decidable invariants in
Definition~\\ref{def:staged}'s sense. This document therefore narrows,
rather than resolves, the earlier framing's gap: the cited results are
now in-repo and in-corpus, but instantiating them to
$\\mathrm{Oracle}(T,R)$ still requires a soundness/completeness argument
for the four sandbox stages that has not been made.
\\begin{speculative}[Fable oracle decidability]\\label{prop:fable-oracle}
$\\mathrm{Oracle}(T,R)$ is a decidable oracle for the correctness question
``does $C$ implement $T$ correctly?'' on the finite test corpus generated
by the harness: it is total, terminating, and labelling (pass or fail
with a structured error from the failing stage).
\\end{speculative}
\\begin{speculative}[Mock membranes as differential oracles]\\label{remark:mockmembranes}
Mock membranes as differential oracles: the mock membrane produces
responses from test fixtures; a real-model run produces a different
response. Running both and comparing (differential oracle) bounds the
false-accept rate: an implementation error that passes the oracle with
the mock response but fails with the real response is caught by the
differential.
\\end{speculative}
Both statements are kernel-admitted (label \\texttt{prop:fable-oracle}:
bare-core and Mathlib, two attempts each, no additional axioms in the
Mathlib lane; label \\texttt{remark:mockmembranes}: bare-core two
attempts, Mathlib one attempt with axiom \\texttt{propext}) -- but kernel
admission here certifies that the statement was formalized as declared,
not that its unresolved dependency on the external Paper~4.7/4.4 result
has been discharged. Until that dependency is either folded into this
corpus or independently re-derived, Proposition~\\ref{prop:fable-oracle}
and Remark~\\ref{remark:mockmembranes} stay in this speculative section,
not beside Section~\\ref{sec:fable-mechanics}'s evidence-bound harness
mechanics.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 12: Hierarchical Merkle Cell Rollups and Write-Ahead Logs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch12 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 12 ;
    doc:label "ch:merkle" ;
    doc:title "Hierarchical Merkle Cell Rollups and Write-Ahead Logs" .

:ch12_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch12 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "Cell rollup" .

:ch12_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch12_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The 10,000-member cell of Chapter~\\ref{ch:cell} needs a
receipt shape that scales to that membership count without linearizing
every member's hash into one flat chain; a two-level Merkle rollup,
implemented in \\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/cell.rs} and
\\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/cell\\_supervise.rs}, is that shape.
\\begin{construction}[Merkle cell rollup]\\label{con:merklecell}
Let $M_{g,i}$ be the terminal hash of member $i$ in group $g$. The Group
Receipt hash is $H_g = \\chainH(\\text{sort}(\\{M_{g,i}\\}))$, and the Cell
Receipt hash is the rolling fold over $G$ group hashes:
$H_{\\text{cell}} = \\chainH(H_1\\,\\Vert\\,H_2\\,\\Vert\\,\\dots\\,\\Vert\\,H_G)$.
\\end{construction}
Kernel-verified in both lanes, label \\texttt{con:merklecell}: bare-core
three attempts; Mathlib two attempts with axiom \\texttt{chainH}.""" .

:ch12_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch12 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "Write-ahead log frames" .

:ch12_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch12_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """Memoized DAG nodes (Chapter~\\ref{ch:topology}) must survive
a crash mid-write without corrupting the cache they journal into; a
length-prefixed, digest-committed frame, implemented in
\\texttt{crates/praxis-synthesis/src/wal.rs}, is what makes a torn write
detectable rather than silently accepted on recovery.
\\begin{definition}[WAL frame]\\label{def:walframe}
A cache frame journaling the memoized DAG nodes is serialized as a
length-prefixed frame:
$\\text{WAL\\_Frame} = \\langle\\,\\text{length},\\ \\chainH(\\text{payload}),\\ \\text{payload}\\,\\rangle$,
allowing recovery to discard torn frames.
\\end{definition}
Kernel-verified in both lanes, label \\texttt{def:walframe}: bare-core
two attempts; Mathlib one attempt with axiom \\texttt{chainH}.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 13: Scope Discipline: The Refusal Register
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch13 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 13 ;
    doc:label "ch:scope" ;
    doc:title "Scope Discipline: The Refusal Register" .

:ch13_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inChapter :ch13 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """The antibody clause of Chapter~\\ref{ch:antibody} bounds
what a claim may assert; this chapter is its dual, naming what this
project explicitly declines to claim or adopt, so that the omission is a
receipted decision rather than a silent gap.""" .

:ch13_s0 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch13 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:title "No path dependency on knhk" .

:ch13_s0_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch13_s0 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{refusal}[No knhk path dependency]\\label{refusal:noknhkpath}
No path dependencies on knhk: verified by live probes, recorded as the
standing reason -- the leanest candidate crate drags
tokio/reqwest/opentelemetry transitively; the mu-kernel ships
property-testing libraries as regular dependencies and uses unsafe
transmutes; the workspace does not build as a whole. Ports of
50--200-line designs, tagged PORT(knhk) with per-item deltas, cost less
than the supply chain. Drift is greppable.
\\end{refusal}
Kernel-verified in both lanes, label \\texttt{refusal:noknhkpath}:
bare-core one attempt; Mathlib two attempts with axioms
\\texttt{knhkTransitiveDeps}, \\texttt{knhk\\_drags\\_disallowed\\_deps},
\\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice}, \\texttt{Quot.sound}. This is
the same discipline named in this repository's own
\\texttt{CLAUDE.md}: ``\\texttt{praxis-synthesis} deps frozen to exactly:
pddl-index, chatman-common, blake3, serde, serde\\_json, thiserror,''
enforced by \\texttt{tests/no\\_llm\\_runtime.rs}.""" .

:ch13_s1 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch13 ;
    doc:order 1 ;
    doc:title "No measured CPU ticks" .

:ch13_s1_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch13_s1 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{refusal}[No fabricated tick measurement]\\label{refusal:nomeasuredticks}
No measured CPU ticks: knhk's own hot receipts carried dummy tick values
behind a hardcoded 4 GHz assumption. Declared deterministic costs (Ticks
as an abstract unit) are the honest model for a planner; rdtsc
instrumentation is a bench follow-up, documented as a divergence, not
smuggled in as a claim.
\\end{refusal}
Kernel-verified in both lanes, label \\texttt{refusal:nomeasuredticks}:
bare-core one attempt; Mathlib two attempts with axioms
\\texttt{declaredTicks}, \\texttt{rdtscTicks}. Read together with
Section~\\ref{sec:wall-time-estimate}'s speculative wall-time estimate, the contrast is
the point: an estimate labelled speculative and an unmeasured quantity
smuggled in as fact are not the same defect, and this refusal is what
keeps this project on the labelled-estimate side of that line for its
own tick accounting.""" .

:ch13_s2 a doc:Section ;
    doc:inChapter :ch13 ;
    doc:order 2 ;
    doc:title "v1 scope, named" .

:ch13_s2_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inSection :ch13_s2 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """\\begin{refusal}[v1 scope]\\label{refusal:v1scope}
v1 scope: beat scheduling, full R1/W1/C1 SLO matrix, quarantine parole,
and supra-cell supervision. Each receipted with salvage: the enum and
one tier transition ship; epoch boundaries already provide the
re-admission cadence beats would refine; quarantined classes await a
parole mechanism; cells supervising cells is the composition step after
this one.
\\end{refusal}
Kernel-verified in both lanes, label \\texttt{refusal:v1scope}: bare-core
one attempt; Mathlib one attempt, no axioms beyond the kernel's own.
This is the same v1-scope discipline the milestone ticket dependency
graph applies at the project level (\\texttt{docs/jira/v26.7.4/tickets/index.md}):
a named boundary with a stated next step, not an unstated one.""" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chapter 14: Conclusion
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ch14 a doc:Chapter ;
    doc:order 14 ;
    doc:label "ch:conclusion" ;
    doc:title "Conclusion" .

:ch14_p0 a doc:Paragraph ;
    doc:inChapter :ch14 ;
    doc:order 0 ;
    doc:register "evidence-bound" ;
    doc:body """This paper showed two halves of one fleet lifecycle. The
admission half (Chapters~\\ref{ch:gap}--\\ref{ch:antibody}) instantiates
the keystone's Comprehension--Verification Gap at one measured scale,
gives a three-part decidable-oracle discipline for trusting concrete
implementations, constructs a branchless byte-level admission sweep with
an exact memory-footprint identity, and states the antibody clause that
bounds every claim -- including this paper's own -- by what its receipt
witness attests. The supervision half
(Chapters~\\ref{ch:topology}--\\ref{ch:scope}) derives, rather than
hand-authors, the stage structure and failure geometry that protect a
running fleet, reports a 10,000-member cell's actual fault-injection
numbers rather than an extrapolation of them, extends the same
gate-and-isolate discipline to a live retrofit applier and to a
spec-driven LLM evaluation harness, and names -- rather than silently
carries -- both its unresolved dependency (the Fable oracle's
decidability argument) and its declined-scope items. Nothing in this
document's speculative chapters and sections (Chapter~\\ref{ch:planetary};
Section~\\ref{sec:fable-speculative}) is mixed with its evidence-bound claims without a
visible marker; per Corollary~\\ref{cor:reflexive} and the reflexive
reading of Chapter~\\ref{ch:antibody}, that partition is this document's
own overclaim set, and it is empty by construction, checkable by the
same SPARQL query that checks any other paper in this series.""" .

